WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.269
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:08.955
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.255 --> 00:00:15.367
Unit twenty-two: The Energy Transition. Today you will state conditions using unless / but for.

4
00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:18.204
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:22.469
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.317 --> 00:00:24.782
Energy transition.

7
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:30.256
Energy transition means the shift from fossil fuels to low-carbon energy.

8
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:33.430
The energy transition will take decades.

9
00:00:34.017 --> 00:00:37.180
Your turn. Say: Energy transition.

10
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:41.580
Energy transition.

11
00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:43.445
Renewables.

12
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.621
Renewables means energy sources such as solar and wind that do not run out.

13
00:00:49.217 --> 00:00:52.563
Renewables are now the cheapest new power in many places.

14
00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:56.081
Your turn. Say: Renewables.

15
00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:00.213
Renewables.

16
00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:02.381
Fossil fuels.

17
00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:07.925
Fossil fuels means coal, oil and gas formed from ancient organisms.

18
00:01:08.517 --> 00:01:11.805
Fossil fuels still supply most of the world's energy.

19
00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:15.533
Your turn. Say: Fossil fuels.

20
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.738
Fossil fuels.

21
00:01:20.483 --> 00:01:21.264
Grid.

22
00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:25.120
Grid means the network that delivers electricity.

23
00:01:25.717 --> 00:01:29.075
A strong grid can carry power from distant wind farms.

24
00:01:29.683 --> 00:01:32.301
Your turn. Say: Grid.

25
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:35.785
Grid.

26
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:37.505
Storage.

27
00:01:38.517 --> 00:01:42.234
Storage means technology that holds energy for later use.

28
00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.117
Storage lets solar power serve the night.

29
00:01:45.717 --> 00:01:48.172
Your turn. Say: Storage.

30
00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:52.139
Storage.

31
00:01:52.883 --> 00:01:53.919
Emissions.

32
00:01:54.917 --> 00:01:58.205
Emissions means greenhouse gases released into the air.

33
00:01:58.817 --> 00:02:00.944
Emissions from transport are rising.

34
00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.493
Your turn. Say: Emissions.

35
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:08.381
Emissions.

36
00:02:09.117 --> 00:02:10.095
Investment.

37
00:02:11.083 --> 00:02:14.290
Investment means money committed to produce future benefit.

38
00:02:14.883 --> 00:02:16.987
Investment in clean power doubled.

39
00:02:17.583 --> 00:02:20.282
Your turn. Say: Investment.

40
00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:24.332
Investment.

41
00:02:25.083 --> 00:02:26.340
Just transition.

42
00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:31.718
Just transition means a shift that treats affected workers and regions fairly.

43
00:02:32.317 --> 00:02:35.013
A just transition retrains coal miners.

44
00:02:35.617 --> 00:02:38.362
Your turn. Say: Just transition.

45
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.538
Just transition.

46
00:02:43.333 --> 00:02:47.123
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.983 --> 00:02:53.550
Visal, solar panels are cheap now. Why isn't everything powered by renewables already?

48
00:02:54.650 --> 00:03:01.886
Unless storage improves, renewables will face limits, because the sun and wind are intermittent.

49
00:03:02.983 --> 00:03:04.217
What else is missing?

50
00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:14.369
Grids. Unless networks are expanded, clean power cannot reach cities. But for large investment, the transition would stall.

51
00:03:15.483 --> 00:03:17.494
And workers in coal regions?

52
00:03:18.583 --> 00:03:25.810
Unless they are retrained, resistance will grow. But for a just transition, political support would collapse.

53
00:03:26.967 --> 00:03:28.467
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:29.317 --> 00:03:31.990
Grammar focus: Unless and but for.

55
00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.407
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:34.117 --> 00:03:37.463
Unless storage improves, renewables will face limits.

57
00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:42.497
Unless networks are expanded, clean power cannot reach cities.

58
00:03:43.383 --> 00:03:46.521
But for large investment, the transition would stall.

59
00:03:47.467 --> 00:03:57.758
Unless = if … not: Unless storage improves (not 'unless storage doesn't improve'). But for + noun = if it were not for: But for investment, the transition would stall.

60
00:03:58.517 --> 00:04:03.340
Be careful! Don't say: Unless storage not improves, limits stay.

61
00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:08.484
Say: Unless storage improves, limits will remain.

62
00:04:08.784 --> 00:04:10.702
Unless already means 'if not'.

63
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.001
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.207
Powering the Transition.

65
00:04:16.507 --> 00:04:23.790
The energy transition aims to replace fossil fuels with low-carbon sources while keeping electricity reliable and affordable.

66
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:32.001
Solar and wind are now cheap, but unless storage improves, renewables will face limits because the sun does not always shine.

67
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:38.947
Unless grids are expanded, power generated in remote regions cannot reach the cities that need it.

68
00:04:39.933 --> 00:04:44.185
But for large public and private investment, none of this progress would have occurred.

69
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:52.273
A just transition, which retrains workers in fossil fuel industries, is essential unless political support is to collapse.

70
00:04:53.367 --> 00:04:57.659
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:58.517 --> 00:05:01.619
blank storage improves, renewables will face limits.

72
00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:05.851
A, Unless. B, Despite. C, Because.

73
00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.063
The answer is A: Unless.

74
00:05:12.217 --> 00:05:14.959
The network that delivers electricity is the blank.

75
00:05:15.259 --> 00:05:18.785
A, storage. B, grid. C, emissions.

76
00:05:22.333 --> 00:05:24.019
The answer is B: grid.

77
00:05:25.183 --> 00:05:28.564
A shift that treats workers fairly is a blank transition.

78
00:05:28.864 --> 00:05:32.100
A, just. B, quick. C, fossil.

79
00:05:35.633 --> 00:05:37.424
The answer is A: just.

80
00:05:38.583 --> 00:05:40.943
'But for investment, it would stall' means…

81
00:05:41.243 --> 00:05:47.717
A, Because of investment, it stalls.. B, Without investment, it would stall.. C, Investment will stall it..

82
00:05:51.267 --> 00:05:54.555
The answer is B: Without investment, it would stall..

83
00:05:55.717 --> 00:05:58.866
According to the reading, a just transition retrains…

84
00:05:59.166 --> 00:06:04.723
A, workers in fossil fuel industries. B, investors. C, engineers abroad.

85
00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:11.984
The answer is A: workers in fossil fuel industries.

86
00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.537
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:17.383 --> 00:06:20.393
What are the obstacles to a fast energy transition?

88
00:06:37.183 --> 00:06:38.637
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:38.937 --> 00:07:05.398
The main obstacles are storage, grids, money and politics. Unless storage improves, renewables cannot supply power when it is dark or still. Grids must be expanded to carry clean power to cities. Large investment is needed, and workers in fossil fuel regions must be treated fairly. Without all four, the transition will be slower than the climate requires.

90
00:07:05.698 --> 00:07:07.616
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:08.517 --> 00:07:12.980
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and unless and but for.

92
00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:17.616
Today's value is sustainable thinking. Choose what can last for the long term.

93
00:07:17.916 --> 00:07:22.054
See you in Unit twenty-three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:22.354 --> 00:07:25.401
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

